# Managed Git, Codex home and system diagnostics

Integration: 14 September 2026. This document describes the implemented contract,
not a claim that every machine has passed live acceptance. Repository validation
results accompany the delivered source tree in `docs/validation-git/`.

## 1. Private installation inputs

Build the PRIVATE Debian installer initrd with exactly these root-owned files:

```
/preseed.env        0600
/git_ed25519       0600   encrypted OpenSSH Ed25519 private key
/git_ed25519.pub   0600   its single-line OpenSSH public key
```

`preseed.env` must contain `PRESEED_GIT_SSH_PASSPHRASE` with the existing private
key's passphrase. It is separate from `PRESEED_PRIMARY_GPG_PASSPHRASE`, which
protects the primary user's managed GPG key. Both are trusted shell assignments;
quote and escape values correctly. The SSH passphrase must be nonempty printable
single-line text, at most 4096 bytes. Public keys initially readable by others
are normalized to private mode by the existing initrd input validator.

The Git SSH passphrase is read ONLY from `/preseed.env`; unlike some older
installer credentials, no kernel-command-line or inherited-environment override
is accepted. Do not use the account password as an implicit fallback.

The key files and `preseed.env` must be built/stored outside the served checkout.
Do not publish the private initrd over the repository's HTTP service. They are
not payload assets and are never fetched from the seed URL. `tools/build.py`
rejects known private input filenames and files beginning with private-key PEM
headers anywhere in the public checkout. This is a guard against mistakes, not
a complete secret scanner: renamed archives, backups and secret assignments in
other files still require review. Serve only the sanitized distribution, not a
working directory containing private media or source-control internals.

The public key must already be authorized for the intended GitLab/GitHub account
or deployment identity, including read permission for the Codex home repository.
Network access to both providers' SSH port 22 must be available where needed.
There is no automatic account registration or credential upload.

### Installer handling

A temporary root-supervised OpenSSH agent proves that the passphrase opens the
key and that the public/private pair matches (`ssh-add -T`). The passphrase is
sent through a private stdin pipe and a sealed anonymous Linux memfd for
SSH_ASKPASS. It is not a process argument, environment value, disk staging file,
unit setting or account metadata field. Encrypted key staging and child process
groups are cleaned on success, failure and catchable interruption.

After the existing primary GPG bootstrap validates the user's key, the passphrase
is encrypted to that GPG public key. The private SSH key itself retains its
original OpenSSH encryption; it is not made passwordless or imported into
`gpg-agent` as an SSH identity. Persistent account state is:

| Path relative to home | Mode | Content |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `.local/share/ssh/private/id_git_ed25519` | 0600 | Original encrypted SSH private key |
| `.local/share/ssh/git-key-passphrase.gpg` | 0600 | GPG-encrypted SSH passphrase |
| `.ssh/id_git_ed25519.pub` | 0600 | Public identity selector |

The managed directories are 0700. Installer helpers are root-owned 0700;
runtime helpers are root-owned executables. Verification and firstboot inspect
metadata and configuration; they never try to unlock the user's GPG key.

Root, a compromised kernel, the private media builder and an attacker able to
read process memory remain trusted boundaries. Anonymous memory can be swapped;
this implementation does not claim hardware-backed key protection or guaranteed
memory erasure. Same-UID processes may use an unlocked agent, and unconfined
same-user code can read user-owned files. The new AppArmor loader domain is a
narrow policy addition, not a replacement for the existing desktop threat model.

## 2. Daily SSH/GPG use

Since the 2026-09-19 session repair, login activates the SSH agent socket but
**does not automatically unlock the encrypted private key**. Unlock is explicit
through `git-ssh unlock`, or requested by the existing `devops` launcher. This
avoids a cold-cache pinentry dialog timing out unattended during session startup.
Existing installer-owned loader wants links are removed on installer reruns;
the loader service itself, its timeout, encryption and error reporting remain.

From a normal terminal inside the primary user's Labwc session:

```sh
git-ssh unlock       # default action; retry after a cancelled GPG prompt
git-ssh status       # public fingerprints only
git-ssh lock         # stop loader, agent service and socket
```

A cold GPG cache prompts for the GPG key's passphrase through pinentry-qt. The
helper decrypts the sealed SSH passphrase directly into SSH_ASKPASS's pipe;
there is no runtime plaintext passphrase file. Once loaded, Git uses the agent.
`git-ssh unlock` is the normal way to decrypt/use the secret: it does not print it.
There is deliberately no convenience command that dumps the passphrase to a
terminal or clipboard. Preserve trusted offline recovery material separately.

Both ordinary terminals and `devops` use
`/run/user/UID/openssh_agent`. `devops` validates this socket and retries the
loader. There is no per-shell `ssh-agent -s` and no `SSH_AGENT_PID` lifecycle.
The package's `ssh-agent.socket` and `ssh-agent.service` receive only Labwc
lifecycle drop-ins; the installer verifies the expected package-unit contract.
An incompatible/missing package unit fails installation instead of selecting an
unreviewed fallback. Global `sockets.target` enablement is disabled; Labwc owns
the explicit wants link. Do not independently enable the vendor socket again.

Cancelling pinentry fails the loader, not the desktop. Logging out stops the
agent and destroys its loaded identity. GPG's existing cache is not globally
flushed, so an immediate re-login can legitimately prompt zero times. A later
unlock can prompt again after cache expiry. Locking the SSH agent does not clear
the GPG cache or retroactively close already-authenticated remote connections.

GitLab and GitHub have exact-host stanzas, public-key-only identity selection,
no forwarding, batch authentication and strict host-key verification. The
private key is outside SSH's default private-key filenames, preventing an
unintended direct-key password prompt. Other hosts retain the existing PKI
include and defaults; the managed Git identity is not placed under `Host *`.

Host Ed25519 keys are pinned in `/etc/ssh/git_known_hosts`. A provider
rotation fails closed: independently confirm the new key from the provider's
authenticated documentation, update that root-owned file and the repository
asset/tests, and rebuild before the next deployment. Never bypass checking with
`StrictHostKeyChecking=no` or trust unverified `ssh-keyscan` output.

Useful nonsecret diagnostics:

```sh
printf '%s\n' "$SSH_AUTH_SOCK"
find -H "$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/openssh_agent" -maxdepth 0 -printf '%u %m %y\n'
ssh-add -l -E sha256
ssh -G gitlab.com
ssh -G github.com
systemctl --user status ssh-agent.socket ssh-agent.service labwc-ssh-key-load.service
journalctl --user -u labwc-ssh-key-load.service --since today
```

Run `ssh -T git@gitlab.com` and `ssh -T git@github.com` only when validating actual
provider authorization. Judge the authentication banner and errors, not an
assumption that a Git-only service provides an interactive shell or exits zero.
Do not send private keys, passphrases or raw unreviewed debug logs for support.

## 3. GitOps policy and aliases

The root-owned engine is `/usr/local/bin/gitops`. Managed aliases are included
from `/etc/gitops/aliases.gitconfig` by the user's `.config/git/config`; existing
`.gitconfig` is not overwritten. An existing user/repository alias with the same
name can override Git's alias resolution. Inspect `git config --show-origin
--get-regexp '^alias\.mcr-'` when diagnosing overrides, or call `gitops` directly.

Configure your real Git commit identity once if it is not already configured:
`git config --global user.name 'Your name'` and
`git config --global user.email 'your-verified-address@example.com'` (replace
both placeholders). No provider email address is invented from the local login
name. Authentication uses the SSH key; commit authorship is a separate setting.

The live policy is `~/.config/gitops/gitops.env`, owned by the user, normally
0600. It is read afresh for each invocation, as DATA, never sourced or evaluated.
Both keys must be present exactly once:

```text
GITOPS_FORKS_MERGE_PROTECTED="AGENTS.override.md .gitlab-ci.yml .github/* debian/* local-settings/"
GITOPS_SALSA_MERGE_PROTECTED="AGENTS.override.md .gitlab-ci.yml .github/* local-settings/"
```

This shortened example is not the complete shipped default. The installed
lists also cover the reference workflow's CI/editor/build/release directories.
The fork list protects `debian/*`; Salsa deliberately does not, allowing Debian
packaging updates. Edit the two lists independently; `""` explicitly disables
protection for that workflow. `gitops config` displays the parsed active lists.
No environment or repository-local file can silently replace this policy.

Bare names match components anywhere in the tree; `Makefile` therefore matches
nested Makefiles too. Patterns containing `/` are repository-rooted. A literal
folder, `folder/` or `folder/*` protects its subtree; rooted `*` spans slashes.
`folder/*` also protects a file/symlink replacing `folder`. Normal fnmatch wildcards
are supported, not Git pathspec magic or a shell. Absolute paths, `..`, `.git`
components and executable shell substitutions are refused. Quote a name inside
the quoted value when it contains spaces.

### Protected-sync semantics

This is explicit upstream TREE synchronization, not a generic three-way merge:
all unprotected paths become the selected upstream snapshot; protected paths keep
the local content AND local absence. Upstream additions to a locally absent
protected path stay absent. Deletion, executable bits, symlinks and file/directory
replacement collisions are handled at the Git tree level. Protect every local
customization that must survive this operation before using `--apply`.

A temporary index constructs the proposed tree; the ordinary index is untouched
during preview. Successful application records parent ancestry and a recovery
ref under `refs/gitops/backups/`, then fast-forwards the checked-out branch.
Protected synchronization never runs `reset --hard`, force-pushes origin, or
executes repository hooks. The separately opted-in mirror role described below
can rewrite mirror refs, but only with explicit object-ID leases and recovery refs.
Only `mcr/*` branches are writable sync targets. Dirty/staged/untracked changes,
ignored-path collisions, sparse/skip-worktree/assume-unchanged state, active Git
operations and submodules are refused for explicit manual review.

Preview is the default for sync/cicd/update/verify-protected-sync. It normally
fetches/prunes `origin` remote-tracking refs, but changes neither HEAD, worktree
nor ordinary index. Use `--no-fetch` to use already-fetched refs offline. The
local GitOps lock prevents overlapping GitOps operations, not a concurrently
running independent editor or arbitrary Git process; final state is rechecked.

Automatic upstream detection prefers the GitLab mirror namespace, then GitHub:
`origin/gitlab/mcr/main`, corresponding local mirror refs, `main`/`master`
variants, then the GitHub variants; finally `upstream/main` or `upstream/master`.
Pass an explicit source revision to avoid ambiguity in multi-mirror repositories.

```sh
git mcr-branch-init          # add missing branches only; no switch or push
git switch mcr/main
gitops config
git mcr-fork-sync origin/gitlab/mcr/main             # preview
git mcr-fork-sync origin/gitlab/mcr/main --apply     # local protected sync
# Salsa uses its own list:
git mcr-salsa-sync origin/gitlab/mcr/main --no-fetch
# Preview or apply mirror -> main -> staging -> release:
git mcr-fork-cicd origin/gitlab/mcr/main
git mcr-fork-cicd origin/gitlab/mcr/main --apply
```

### Complete managed command families

| Aliases | Behavior |
| --- | --- |
| `mcr-branch-init`, `mcr-fork-init`, `mcr-salsa-init` | Create missing main/staging/release/feature-initial branches from optional revision or HEAD; preserve existing refs |
| `mcr-branch-create NAME` | Create/switch a validated `mcr/*` branch |
| `mcr-branch-delete NAME` | Delete a merged non-core local branch only |
| `mcr-branch-show`, `mcr-repo-status` | Status, branches and remotes |
| `mcr-repo-clone SSH-URL [DEST]` or `mcr-repo-clone <gh|glab> NAMESPACE REPO` | Clone an existing GitHub/GitLab repository through SSH |
| `mcr-repo-mirror <gh|glab> NAMESPACE [--apply]` | Preview or configure a private, push-only promotion mirror on the other provider |
| `mcr-repo-mirror <gh|glab> del [--apply]` | Preview or remove the local mirror remote; never delete a provider repository |
| `mcr-repo-sync` | Non-force push of the current `mcr/*` branch to origin |
| `mcr-branch-push` | Fast-forward promotion through main/staging/release, then atomic remote push |
| `mcr-tag-create TAG`, `mcr-tag-push TAG` | Create annotated local tag, or explicitly push that tag |
| `mcr-tag-delete TAG`, `mcr-tag-show` | Local tag deletion, or listing; no remote deletion |
| `mcr-{fork,salsa}-sync [SOURCE]` | Preview/apply protected sync of the current `mcr/*` branch |
| `mcr-{fork,salsa}-verify-protected-sync [SOURCE]` | Always preview; `--apply` does not turn verification into mutation |
| `mcr-{fork,salsa}-cicd [SOURCE]` | Preview/apply protected mirror -> main -> staging -> release chain |
| `mcr-{fork,salsa}-update [SOURCE]` | Same chain; on apply, also check an existing patch series on main |
| `mcr-{fork,salsa}-push [SOURCE] --apply` | Apply chain/check existing patches, then atomic push of all three refs |
| `mcr-{fork,salsa}-check`, `mcr-{fork,salsa}-test` | Check patch series applicability in a disposable index; no compilation/tests of application binaries |

The chain preserves each branch's protected files and restores the initial
checked-out branch. Promotion is NOT a multi-branch local rollback transaction:
if a later step or remote push fails, earlier local commits can remain, with
recovery refs and no force reset. Inspect status/history, resolve the specific
problem, then rerun. The remote multi-ref push requests atomic behavior; a server
that does not support it causes failure, not a non-atomic fallback.

```sh
git for-each-ref --format='%(refname) %(objectname)' refs/gitops/backups/
# To inspect an earlier state without discarding work:
git branch recovery-review refs/gitops/backups/REPLACE_WITH_RECORDED_REF
```

Patch checking reads `debian/patches/series` preferentially, or the existing
`patches/release/series` convention. It accepts ordinary quilt names with an
optional `-pN`, applies sequentially to a disposable index, and leaves the
worktree unchanged. Unsupported options fail. A standalone check requires a
series; chain update/push records when none exists rather than pretending a
patch test ran. Remote project creation is confined to explicit mirror setup
below. These commands do not generate age keys or start build pipelines.

### Push-only GitHub / GitLab mirrors

Prepare the three local promotion branches with `git mcr-branch-init`, then run
as the desktop user with the provider CLI already authenticated:

```sh
# Example: origin is on GitLab, destination is a GitHub user or organization.
git mcr-repo-mirror gh YOUR_NAMESPACE             # preview; no remote writes
git mcr-repo-mirror gh YOUR_NAMESPACE --apply     # explicit destructive consent
# Reverse direction, with a nested GitLab group:
git mcr-repo-mirror glab YOUR_GROUP/SUBGROUP
# Remove the LOCAL role only; the provider project and its data remain:
git mcr-repo-mirror gh del --apply
```

Use only the example matching origin's provider; origin and mirror cannot have
the same provider. The destination repository basename comes from origin's
single canonical push URL. The named remote is `mirror-gh` or `mirror-glab`.
An existing mirror with a different URL is refused instead of silently retargeted.
Preview reports the proposed full ref IDs and deletions. `--apply` creates a
missing project privately, or verifies and retains an existing project's
visibility. Auth/API errors are not treated as evidence that a project is
missing. Namespace, repository identity, and provider host are checked.
GitHub organizations and the authenticated user's namespace are supported;
GitLab supports owned user namespaces and nested groups with write access.

Configuration makes the remote's default branch `mcr/main`, and leaves exactly
`mcr/main`, `mcr/staging`, and `mcr/release` as its branches with no tags.
**Applying setup to an existing repository can rewrite branches and delete all
other remote branches and tags.** Read the preview first. Local tags, feature
branches, origin refs/refspecs, and origin's default branch are not removed or
retargeted. This does not create a conventional all-refs `git --mirror` backup.

Before changing existing remote refs, their objects are fetched into unique
local `refs/gitops/mirror-backups/...` recovery refs and their identities checked.
Pushes use pinned source object IDs, a per-ref `--force-with-lease` expectation,
and `--atomic`; there is no plain `--force` or non-atomic fallback. A concurrent
remote change fails instead of overwriting unreviewed work. Provider protection
rules remain in force: the tool does not unprotect branches to force success.
Recovery refs remain local; retain the local repository when investigating a
failure. Inspect them with:

```sh
git for-each-ref --format='%(refname) %(objectname)' refs/gitops/mirror-backups/
```

Setting the provider default branch and bootstrapping that branch necessarily
precede deleting an old default branch. These API operations and later Git
pushes **are not one transaction**. Failed setup can leave a private project or
an updated default branch; the error explicitly requires inspection before retry.
Local mirror configuration is published only after remote ref verification.
Repeated successful setup preserves the same local configuration.

Successful origin branch-push workflows (`mcr-repo-sync`, `mcr-branch-push`,
and `mcr-{fork,salsa}-push`) subsequently send the three promotion tips to
configured mirrors. They never send local tags or feature/upstream branches to
mirrors. Explicit tag-push aliases still target origin only. A mirror failure
makes the command fail, but an already successful origin push cannot be rolled
back across providers. The output states this partial outcome. Setup normalization
prunes remote extras; ordinary subsequent branch updates leave unrelated tags
untouched until setup is explicitly reapplied.

### Terminal reports and exact commit identities

All managed aliases print their invocation and outcome. Repository commands
print before/after state, full changed-ref IDs, and detailed commit information;
Git's successful stdout and stderr are retained for mutating/network commands.
Output includes operation start/completion, branch/ref changes, repository object
format, full native commit ID, tree, parents, author/date, and subject where a
commit exists. There is no invented commit identity for an unborn repository.
Output is captured per subprocess, not promised as byte-by-byte live progress.
Control characters are escaped and URL user-info is redacted in reports.

The extra `SHA256(stored commit object)` value is calculated as:

```text
SHA256(b"commit " + ASCII(decimal_payload_length) + b"\0" + raw_commit_payload)
```

It fingerprints the complete canonical stored commit object. In a SHA-1-format
repository the native commit ID is still SHA-1; the fingerprint does NOT claim
to convert that repository or its embedded tree/parent IDs into Git's SHA-256
object format. In a native SHA-256 repository the fingerprint matches its native
commit ID. Both formats are covered by the offline fixtures. Failures return
nonzero and print the state to inspect rather than a success message.

## 4. Codex home

All desktop profiles use:

```text
DEVOPS_CODEX_REPOSITORY_URL=git@gitlab.com:computes/misc/codex-home.git
DEVOPS_CODEX_REPOSITORY_BRANCH=mcr/main
```

There is no `DEVOPS_CODEX_REPOSITORY_COMMIT` input. The private installer agent
performs a full-history, single-branch SSH clone and proves the symbolic branch
is `mcr/main` tracking `origin/mcr/main`. The main `.git/HEAD`, repository history
and branch tracking are retained in `/data/codex/usr`; no detached checkout or
SHA pin is used. `home/memories` is account-owned runtime state; its `.git`
metadata, if any, is neither synthesized nor required by the wrapper.

The existing Codex binary/version/archive verification, publication checks,
ownership and runtime sandbox layout remain. Moving-branch deployment is
intentionally not byte-for-byte reproducible across different upstream commits.
The existing-state validator still refuses unexpected immutable home content,
while allowing account-owned `home/config.toml` to change after login.
No automatic login-time pull/reset is introduced.

The host can use SSH for provisioning and GitOps, but the Codex wrapper continues
to strip `SSH_AUTH_SOCK` and `SSH_AGENT_PID`. This change does not grant the Codex
sandbox access to the desktop signing agent or the user's managed SSH/GPG files.

## 5. debugsys

Run `debugsys` from a terminal. The wrapper enters `sudo -i` and executes the
root-owned collector with isolated Python startup and a controlled environment.
No new Perl/Moo dependency or compilation is needed for these new components.
Python's standard library and small POSIX/initramfs shell helpers are used.

The multi-select menu accepts numbers, commas, spaces and ranges such as `1,6-9`.
Selections are shown before execution; overlapping report categories are deduped.

| Entry | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| 1 | Hardware inventory and potential tuning review |
| 2 | System health, services and boot debugging |
| 3 | Prepare next-boot initramfs capture and rebuild all images |
| 4 | Boot timeline and captured early-boot evidence |
| 5 | Remove managed initramfs hooks and rebuild all images |
| 6 | Labwc, graphics, audio and desktop-session evidence |
| 7 | AppArmor, process isolation and security state |
| 8 | Storage, memory pressure and device health |
| 9 | Network, drivers and connectivity state |
| 10 | SSH/GPG/Git integration metadata, not secret contents |
| 11 | Comprehensive overview plus ALL read-only categories |
| 12 | Capture status and existing report locations |
| 0 | Exit |

Entry 11 NEVER arms/rebuilds/removes boot hooks. Selecting both 3 and 5 is
rejected. Boot-changing actions require typing `ARM` or `REMOVE`, or the explicit
CLI `--yes`. There is no automatic reboot or hardware tuning.

```sh
debugsys report                                    # all read-only categories
debugsys report --categories hardware storage
debugsys report --categories desktop security credentials
debugsys status
debugsys boot-enable --yes                         # explicit boot change
# Reboot normally only after success; inspect the generated report after boot.
debugsys boot-disable --yes                        # explicit removal/rebuild
```

### Evidence and overview

Reports are under `__INSTALLER_LOG_DEBUGSYS_DIR__/UTC-report-ID/`, root-only directories 0700
and files 0600. Each contains `OVERVIEW.md`, `TUNING.md`, `manifest.json` and an
`evidence/` directory. The overview links evidence and highlights inventory,
CPU/power policy, memory/storage/pressure, kernel/service problems, desktop,
security and networking. Tuning notes are evidence-linked review prompts, NOT
benchmarks or claims that a setting should be changed on every machine.

Collection covers CPU topology/cpufreq/cpuidle, DMI and firmware, PCIe/AER,
USB/runtime power, thermal sensors/battery, vulnerabilities, GPU/DRM/backlight,
audio/session services, systemd/journals, process names/namespaces/hardening,
block topology/filesystems/SMART/NVMe, zram/zswap/THP/NUMA/PSI, network routes,
listeners, DNS and NIC driver/offload settings. A subset of commands runs as the
identified desktop user with a validated runtime bus/socket; root orchestrates
and reads system evidence. Missing hardware, optional programs, a missing desktop
session, denied reads, timeouts, output truncation and limits are recorded, not
reported as successful checks.

Commands have bounded time/output and process-group cleanup. Defaults: 2 MiB
per command, 256 KiB per file, 96 MiB total evidence, 4096 file candidates per
category, 15-minute report scheduling budget; automatic boot reports have a
3-minute budget. New reports require 224 MiB free report-filesystem space. These
limits are not an assurance against a kernel device stuck in uninterruptible
I/O; later probes may be skipped. Kernel/proc/sysfs evidence is read-only. SMART
queries avoid intentionally waking a standby disk. No stress tests, writes to
tuning knobs, module reloads, firmware updates or packet captures occur.

The manifest records command/source, outcome, byte count and SHA-256 for each
evidence file. Common credential assignments, bearer/authorization fields,
URL credentials, PEM private-key blocks and terminal control sequences are
redacted where recognized. This is best-effort: logs can contain secrets in
unrecognized formats, hostnames, serials, MAC/IP addresses and user activity.
Review before copying/sharing. The collector never opens private-key bodies,
GPG secret material, `/preseed.env` or `/proc/*/environ`, and never decrypts GPG.
There is no automatic upload or deletion of old reports; manage retention
explicitly, especially while boot capture is enabled.

### Early-boot capture and rollback

Inactive templates are shipped by default. Arming installs ONLY these managed
paths and sets `/var/lib/debugsys/armed`:

```
/etc/initramfs-tools/hooks/debugsys
/etc/initramfs-tools/scripts/init-top/debugsys
/etc/initramfs-tools/scripts/init-premount/debugsys
/etc/initramfs-tools/scripts/local-top/debugsys
/etc/initramfs-tools/scripts/local-premount/debugsys
/etc/initramfs-tools/scripts/local-bottom/debugsys
/etc/initramfs-tools/scripts/init-bottom/debugsys
```

Both enable and removal run exactly `update-initramfs -u -k all`, then use Debian
`linux-version list` to inspect EVERY existing installed-kernel image in `/boot`
with `lsinitramfs` for the expected presence
or absence of the helper and six scripts. Backup/orphan rescue images, which
`update-initramfs -u -k all` does not update, are retained and listed in the
transaction record. Booting an old rescue image may still run its old hooks.
Only files with the managed marker are
replaced/deleted; an administrator's unrelated hook is never overwritten.

Each phase attempts a bounded snapshot in `/run/initramfs/debugsys`. The enabled
`debugsys-boot-report.service` consolidates it once per boot ID into a root-only
report after real-root startup. Capture persists across boots until explicitly
removed. The scripts do not enable shell tracing or collect raw key files.
They are best-effort and must not stop normal boot on an ordinary probe failure.

This is not a capture of firmware, the bootloader or every kernel event before
initramfs starts. A crash before writable `/run`, a failure before switching
root, or reboot after failure can prevent automatic persistence. In an initramfs
rescue shell, preserve `/run/initramfs/debugsys` manually to trusted private
media when available; do not assume the post-boot collector has run.

Before each hook transaction, root-only before-images, paths/modes and rebuild
logs are recorded under `/var/lib/debugsys/transaction-UTC-ID/`. On failure or a
catchable interruption the previous hooks are restored, ALL images rebuilt
again and rollback contents verified. If rollback is incomplete, the command
explicitly says not to reboot. Use the transaction's `before.json`, saved files
and logs to restore ONLY those exact paths; then rerun `update-initramfs -u -k all`
and inspect every image. Do not blindly run unreviewed shell code from a report.
A power loss or SIGKILL cannot be transactionally caught; retain a known-good
boot/rescue path and verify images before rebooting after an interrupted change.

Removing hooks also disables future consolidation but retains past reports and
recovery records. Evidence already present in the current boot's `/run` remains
until reboot; it is not a sign that rebuilt future images still contain hooks.

## 6. Deployment acceptance (real target required)

Use a disposable machine/VM before fleet rollout. Verify both btrfs and f2fs late
integration as relevant to the deployment. A successful static parser is not an
AppArmor enforce-mode, SSH-server, installed-package or reboot test.

Check a cold/warm/cancelled GPG unlock, fingerprint consistency, retry via
`devops`, exact socket ownership/mode, normal terminal Git operations and agent
termination at Labwc logout. Confirm SSH effective configuration on both hosts
and real repository authorization. Ensure the main Codex checkout has symbolic
`mcr/main`, the expected origin and upstream, and the sandbox has no agent socket
capability. Exercise protected sync on a disposable remote before production.

On the target, inspect loader-related AppArmor denials and validate the narrowly
scoped policy in enforce mode. Do not add blanket home/filesystem/network rules
to suppress errors. New package/library/pinentry behavior can require narrowly
justified policy changes. Existing developer-tool permissions are not broadened
by this integration.

Finally, arm initramfs capture, verify all images, boot, read the combined report,
remove the hooks, verify all images again and boot once more. Check that unrelated
hooks and reports survive. Never treat mocked hook tests as proof of bootability.

## 7. Design references

The supplied *Managed Git SSH Agent Integration Research Report* provided the
SSH/GPG separation and Labwc lifecycle blueprint. Its example `/git-ssh/...`
paths were replaced with the requested root-initrd paths. The supplied GitOps
and Debian hardware archives informed alias families, protection defaults and
inventory coverage; neither was installed wholesale.

Primary references checked for implementation semantics and host keys:

- OpenSSH client configuration: https://manpages.debian.org/testing/openssh-client/ssh_config.5.en.html
- OpenSSH add/agent behavior: https://manpages.debian.org/testing/openssh-client/ssh-add.1.en.html
- GnuPG cache/pinentry policy: https://www.gnupg.org/documentation/manuals/gnupg/Agent-Options.html
- GitHub official host keys: https://docs.github.com/en/authentication/keeping-your-account-and-data-secure/githubs-ssh-key-fingerprints
- GitLab official host keys: https://gitlab.com/help/instance_configuration#ssh-host-keys-fingerprints
- Debian initramfs hooks and phases: https://manpages.debian.org/testing/initramfs-tools-core/initramfs-tools.7.en.html
- Linux CPUFreq policy evidence: https://www.kernel.org/doc/html/latest/admin-guide/pm/cpufreq.html
